<!--
  /elements — the built-in component library: what it is and what it costs,
  in that order. Internals live at the bottom for the curious.
-->
<column class="doc" gap="5">
  <p class="eyebrow">Components</p>
  <h1>Elements</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    SUI's component library is built into the language. A dialog, a combobox,
    a table, a tab strip — they are tags, written the way you write HTML, with
    the keyboard handling, focus management, ARIA and theming already correct.
    Nothing to install, import, or register.
  </p>
  <p>
    Use them like HTML and you are done — <a href="/components">the browser</a>
    shows every one running. The rest of this page is for when you want to know
    what they cost: almost nothing, because most of them stop existing at
    compile time.
  </p>
  <h2>Under the hood: four mechanisms</h2>

  <h2>1 — Native, plus a skin</h2>
  <p>
    Seventeen of them are the browser's own. A dialog is
    <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code>; a disclosure is <code>&lt;details&gt;</code>; a
    menu rides <code>[popover]</code> and the top layer. We add a stylesheet and
    nothing else.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>These ship zero JavaScript.</strong> The focus trap, the ESC key, the
    top-layer stacking, the ARIA — the platform already implements all of it,
    correctly, and has for years. Most libraries reimplement it in 40&nbsp;kB and
    get the edge cases wrong.
  </p>
  <code-block code={code.native}></code-block>
  <p sx="text:sm fg:muted-foreground">
    button · checkbox · details · dialog · dropzone · fieldset · hr · input ·
    layout · meter · progress · prose · select · skeleton · status-dot · table ·
    textarea
  </p>

  <h2>2 — Element mode: the tag compiles away</h2>
  <p>
    Where the platform has no element — a combobox, a toast region, a breadcrumb
    — we define one. It is authored in <code>.sui</code> like anything else, and
    then the compiler <strong>inlines it</strong>: the tag becomes plain markup
    plus a <code>use:</code> action, and <em>no custom element is registered.</em>
  </p>
  <p>
    So <code>&lt;combobox&gt;</code> costs you a <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> and one
    action. Not a class, not a registry entry, not a definition in the custom
    element registry, not a microtask on upgrade. You write a component; you ship
    markup.
  </p>
  <code-block code={code.elementMode}></code-block>

  <p sx="text:sm fg:muted-foreground">
      alert · avatar · badge · breadcrumb · card · chip · code-block ·
      collapsible · combobox · context-menu · dropdown · empty-state ·
      error-boundary · error-state · field · hover-card · icon · label ·
      menu-item · navbar · page · pagination · popover · radio · radio-group ·
      scroll-area · separator · slider · spinner · switch · tab · tab-list ·
      tab-panel · tabs · theme-picker · toast · toast-region · toggle ·
      toggle-group · tooltip
  </p>

  <h2>3 — <code>use:</code> is where the behavior lives</h2>
  <p>
    Every element above gets its keyboard handling, its roving focus, its ARIA
    sync from an action — a plain function over the element. It is not a
    privileged internal mechanism: <a href="/behavior">the action you write looks
    exactly like the ones we write</a>, because they are the same thing.
  </p>
  <code-block code={code.action}></code-block>

  <h2>4 — Layout keywords</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="/layout"><code>&lt;row&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;column&gt;</code>,
    <code>&lt;grid&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;stack&gt;</code>,
    <code>&lt;center&gt;</code></a> are lowered by the preprocessor rather than
    element mode, for one reason: they take <em>dynamic</em> values, and element
    mode forbids those. It is the only mechanism that exists because of a
    constraint rather than a preference.
  </p>
  <code-block code={code.keyword}></code-block>

  <h2>A variant is a prop, not a component</h2>
  <p>
    This is where most libraries leak. A sheet is not a new component — it is a
    dialog that arrives from the side. An alert dialog is not a new component —
    it is a dialog with a role. A rail is not a new component — it is a
    <code>&lt;navbar&gt;</code> on its side.
  </p>
  <p>
    Collapsing those into props is what keeps the surface at forty-eight instead
    of ninety, and it means <strong>the model cannot pick the wrong one, because
    there is not a wrong one to pick.</strong>
  </p>
  <code-block code={code.variant}></code-block>

  <h2>Anatomy is classes</h2>
  <p>
    Light DOM, so every internal is reachable, inspectable, and documented. There
    is no <code>::part()</code> to expose, no shadow boundary to fight, and no
    "how do I style the inner span" issue thread. The element's classes
    <em>are</em> its API, published as CEM.
  </p>
  <code-block code={code.anatomy}></code-block>

  <alert tone="info">
    <strong>Every element, every state, every theme — verified on every commit.</strong>
    Forty-eight conformance specs render each element across all nine themes and
    drive the interactive ones in a real browser. The consistency claim is not a
    promise; it is a test that fails.
  </alert>

  <row class="page-nav" gap="2">
    <a class="btn" href="/getting-started">← Getting started</a>
    <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/layout">Layout →</a>
  </row>
</column>
